      "math_academy_id": "e-10712",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}}$ given that $x > 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}} = \\sqrt {\\dfrac {32x^5} {2x^3}} = \\sqrt {16x^2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt {16x^2}&= \\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt {4^2x^2}&= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt {(4x)^2}&= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t|4x| &=\\[5pt]\n|4| \\cdot |x| &=\\[5pt]\n4 \\cdot |x|\n\t}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n4 \\cdot |x| &=\\[5pt]\n4 \\cdot x &=\\[5pt]\n4x.\t\n\t}\n$$",
